Whole-slide histopathology experiments can fail before model fitting through annotation ambiguity, contaminated patches, patient leakage, color variability, incompatible artifacts, and validation choices that leak into the test set. This work presents an automated, reproducibility-oriented workflow for cancer detection and segmentation in digital pathology and evaluates it on the DIAGSET prostate histopathology dataset. The workflow converts slide and annotation pairs into patch datasets, performs manual-review-guided graph cleaning, creates patient-disjoint training, validation, and test splits, applies stain normalization from training-derived state, reduces only the training split by embedding-based smart sampling, trains a fixed registry of segmentation models, optimizes a two-stream ensemble on validation patients, and evaluates the frozen recipe on held-out test patients. In the DIAGSET experiment, 419 valid image--annotation cases yielded 1,186,778 retained patches across 393 patients; the test set contained 79 patients and 387,325 patches. No strategy was uniformly best. The non-normalized setting obtained the highest pixel-level micro Dice (0.8415; 95\% confidence interval, 0.7767--0.8987) and patient-level macro Dice (0.7540; 0.6710--0.8356), Ruifrok obtained the highest area under the receiver operating characteristic curve estimate (0.9579) and positive-patient Dice (0.7985; 0.7189--0.8688), and Macenko obtained the highest patch balanced accuracy before patient suppression (0.9009). However, all settings produced false positives in at least 11 of 41 negative test patients. The results support an auditable experimental protocol, while showing that clinical interpretation remains limited by false-positive control and by evaluation on a single dataset.
